OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGLEADER OF STRANGEWAYS
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGINQUIRY ANNOUNCED
OL,5, Home Secretary David Waddington has
OL,6, announced that the inquiry into the
OL,7, Strangeways prison riot will be led by
OL,8, appeal court judge Lord Justice Woolf.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FHe told MPs in the House of Commons
OL,11,Fthat the inquiry report would be
OL,12,Fpublished.
OL,14,FMeanwhile, one prisoner still holding
OL,15,Fout at the jail has denied any inmates
OL,16,Fare being prevented from surrendering.
OL,18,FJail authorities say two inmates gave
OL,19,Fup today and they know how many remain
OL,20,Finside the jail but will not give
OL,21,Fdetails for "operational reasons".
OL,23,D]FFromG05-04-90F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49605
SC,0005
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775420107
OL,1,Vk4                               G5/22
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGPRISON OFFICERS SAY
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pG"MORE STAFF NEEDED"
OL,4,D
OL,5, The National Executive of the Prison
OL,6, Officers Association has warned the
OL,7, Government of more jail problems unless
OL,8, staff numbers are increased urgently.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FThe prison officers were at the Home
OL,11,FOffice for a meeting arranged before
OL,12,Fthe riots started at Strangeways.
OL,14,FPOA general secretary David Evans re-
OL,15,Fiterated their claim that evidence of
OL,16,Fimpending problems was given to the
OL,17,Fgovernor at Strangeways before the riot
OL,19,FMeanwhile the governor has stressed the
OL,20,Fdeath of a prison officer on duty when
OL,21,Friots began was not caused by the riots
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG05-04-90F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49606
SC,0006
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775420107
OL,1,Vk4                               G6/22
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGOFFICER HELD HOSTAGE AT
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGDURHAM PRISON
OL,4,D
OL,5, A prison officer who was taken hostage
OL,6, at knifepoint in Durham prison by two
OL,7, inmates has been released.
OL,8,F
OL,9,FThe officer was seized yesterday as he
OL,10,Funlocked a cell. He was later exchanged
OL,11,Ffor another officer and one inmate gave
OL,12,Fhimself up during the swap.
OL,14,FThe Home Office requested a news
OL,15,Fblackout for fear that media coverage
OL,16,Fmight endanger the officer's life. One
OL,17,Fprisoner had been jailed for murder.
OL,19,FIt was lifted at about 0830 after the
OL,20,Fsecond man was overpowered. Two prison
OL,21,Fofficers were slightly hurt.
OL,23,D]FFromG05-04-90F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49607
SC,0007
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775420107
OL,1,Vk4                               G7/22
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGBUSH AND GORBACHEV SET
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGDATES FOR SUMMIT
OL,5, US President Bush and Soviet President
OL,6, Gorbachev have agreed to hold a summit
OL,7, in the United States from 30 May to
OL,8, 3 June, it has been announced.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FThe summit dates were disclosed in
OL,11,Fsimultaneous statements by the White
OL,12,FHouse and the Soviet news agency TASS.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FThey did not say where it will be held
OL,15,Fbut it is thought it will be Washington
OL,17,FMeanwhile, Secretary of State James
OL,18,FBaker and Soviet Foreign Minister
OL,19,FEduard Shevardnadze are preparing for a
OL,20,Fsecond day of talks in Washington.
OL,21,F
OL,23,D]FFromG05-04-90F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49608
SC,0008
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775420107
OL,1,Vk4                               G8/22
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGS AFRICA TALKS BOYCOTTED
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGBY TRIBAL LEADERS
OL,4,D
OL,5, Four of South Africa's non-independent
OL,6, homeland leaders have boycotted the
OL,7, government's first attempts to prepare
OL,8, a new constitution for the country.
OL,9,FG
OL,10,FBut Chief Buthelezi, leader of KwaZulu,
OL,11,Fand the leader of QwaQwa homeland, did
OL,12,Fmeet President de Klerk in Cape Town.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FThe talks are aimed at ending rival
OL,15,Fblack violence and dismantling white
OL,16,Fminority rule in those homelands which
OL,17,Fdo not have nominal independence.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FLater the government will have its
OL,20,Ffirst official talks with the ANC when
OL,21,FMr de Klerk meets Nelson Mandela.
OL,23,D]FFromG05-04-90F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49609
SC,0009
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775420107
OL,1,Vk4                               G9/22
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGCOUP IN SOUTH
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGAFRICAN HOMELAND
OL,5, The government of the South African
OL,6, homeland Venda has been overthrown in
OL,7, a military coup.
OL,9,FReports say the coup was led by Colonel
OL,10,FGabriel Ramushwana, deputy chief of the
OL,11,FVenda defence force, who said he would
OL,12,Flead the territory until it was re-
OL,13,Fincorporated into South Africa.
OL,15,FVenda is one of four nominally
OL,16,Findependent black homelands created
OL,17,Funder South Africa's apartheid policy.
OL,19,FThe coup comes a month after one in the
OL,20,FSouth African homeland of Ciskei.
OL,21,F
OL,22,D]F This Ceefax page is fromG05-04-90
OL,23,D]F Much more at: teletextarchive.com
